$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: General Procedure for Reductive Aminations.
Benzaldehyde (0.4 mL, 3.9 mmol) and propan-1-amine (0.48 mL, 5.9 mmol)
were dissolved in 3.3 mL of THF (1.2 M), each in separate vials. A sample
of 1.5 mL of methanol (37.2 mmol) was divided between the two vials to
create equal volumes. After flushing the column with THF, the two
reagents were combined, using the Vapourtec software, each at a flow rate
of 0.2 mL/min. The entirety of the output was collected (including one
column volume of pure solvent following the reaction to ensure full
elution of product), washed with distilled water (20 mL), and washed
three times with dichloromethane (15 mL × 3). The combined organic phase
was dried over Na2SO4, filtered, and dried using rotary evaporation.
N-Benzylpropanamine. N-Benzylpropanamine was prepared according to
general procedure for reductive amination with benzaldehyde (0.416 g)
dissolved in a mixture of MeOH (0.8 mL) and THF (3.3 mL), and propylamine
(0.348 g) was dissolved in a mixture of MeOH (0.7 mL) and THF (3.3 mL).
After silica gel column chromatography (1:5 ethyl acetate−n-hexane), pure
N-propylbenzyl amine (0.468 g) was obtained as a colorless oil (80%).
N-Methylbenzylamine. N-Methylbenzylamine was prepared according to
general procedure for reductive amination with benzaldehyde (0.56 g)
dissolved in a mixture of MeOH (1.72 mL) and THF (4.41 mL), and to methyl
amine (5.813 mL of a 2 M THF solution) was added 0.33 mL of MeOH. After
silica gel column chromatography (1:5 ethyl acetate−n-hexane), pure
N-methylbenzyl amine (0.41 g) was obtained as a colorless oil (64%). 1H
NMR: 7.35 (m, 5H), 4.30 (dd, J = 12, <4 Hz, 2H), 3.5 (dd, J = 16, 12 Hz,
1H), 2.43 (m, 3H).
